Z||000006||88formula||04-30-2001||10:00 PM||||I somewhat disagree with the ported head theory. There are many Chevy small blocks out there with ported heads that are daily drivers. I don't recommend that you enlarge the ports but what you should do is blend the bowl area and polish and blend the exhaust ports. Don’t make them bigger just blend them to smooth the flow. By doing this you will actually pick up more torque and low-end power. The heads are second in importance only to the camshaft on effect on engine power. Your valve job should also be a performance three angle or more valve job and not the standard 1-2 angle hand grind that most people get. If you spend your time in these areas, pick a good camshaft, and get headers with matching pipes and mufflers your engine will really make some torque, not loose it.<P>The GM 12363220 camshaft and the Crane PowerMax H-260-2 camshafts are probably the two best street camshafts for this engine. If you want me to test a camshaft with a particular engine combination on Dyno2000 and give you approximate 0-60 and quarter mile times with Dragstrip2000 e-mail me and I will do that. <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||05-01-2001||01:12 PM||||You wouldnt by chance mean Jack and Sons at either<BR>2695 S Decatur or 7010 W russell???||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||05-01-2001||02:10 PM||||Actually, polishing the exhaust ports will help even a daily street car. By polishing the exhaust ports you reduce the chance of carbon build up. It's harder carbon to stick to a smooth surface.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||05-01-2001||04:51 PM||||Speaking of ignition:<P>I thought I heard that the GM ignition really starts to fall off above around 4500 rpms.  The MSD 6AL along with their coil takes care of giving strong spark throughout the entire rpm range.<P>Any thoughts on this?  Any real life experience (documented)?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wannabe||05-01-2001||05:28 PM||||A real life experience, as told to me by the person who experienced it: Adding an MSD boxe made a noticable improvement in accelleration, but in that case, the car was turbocharged. The factory coil is marginal above 4500 without mods, and so would be completely inadequate in a turbo application.  Most everyone I've spoken to about it says they noticed an improvement with even an Accel coil upgrade.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||05-01-2001||07:47 PM||||Didn't say it would be useless, just that it doesn't ADD horsepower. Just frees up some that is lost due to mis-fire. If your coil is cooked, and not ingniting the charge in the upper RPMs, you will lose power. A higher-grade coil will free this up for you. I've never seen or driven a car that made a noticeable difference, but do have a few friends who said that they did. I won't argue that it is probably necesary when tuning an engine for performance, but I don't believe that a coil alone will help you all that much.||||reg||6
